\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Mean simulated population over time (three replicates, simulation years 0--16, i.e.\ 2005--2021), one panel per schedule. Reading left to right, top to bottom: baseline; EU protection 2007; hunting ban 2016; intervention cull 2021; habitat loss; anthropogenic food; combined realistic. The two food-driven schedules (anthropogenic food and combined realistic) are the only ones that produce a sustained rise, while the baseline and the policy-only schedules stay near 6000 bears across the window.}}{32}{figure.4.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch4_population_all}{{4.1}{32}{Mean simulated population over time (three replicates, simulation years 0--16, i.e.\ 2005--2021), one panel per schedule. Reading left to right, top to bottom: baseline; EU protection 2007; hunting ban 2016; intervention cull 2021; habitat loss; anthropogenic food; combined realistic. The two food-driven schedules (anthropogenic food and combined realistic) are the only ones that produce a sustained rise, while the baseline and the policy-only schedules stay near 6000 bears across the window}{figure.4.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Baseline mortality structure (mean per year, three replicates). Top left: total deaths; top right: starvation deaths; bottom left: danger deaths; bottom right: old-age deaths. Starvation accounts for the large majority of mortality, which is the reason food availability is the most effective lever in the campaign.}}{33}{figure.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch4_baseline_mortality}{{4.2}{33}{Baseline mortality structure (mean per year, three replicates). Top left: total deaths; top right: starvation deaths; bottom left: danger deaths; bottom right: old-age deaths. Starvation accounts for the large majority of mortality, which is the reason food availability is the most effective lever in the campaign}{figure.4.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Births versus total deaths over time (mean per year, three replicates). Top row: baseline births (left) and baseline total deaths (right). Bottom row: anthropogenic-food births (left) and anthropogenic-food total deaths (right). Under the food driver, births stay high while deaths fall, and it is this widening gap between births and deaths that produces the sustained population increase seen in Figure~\ref {fig:ch4_population_all}.}}{33}{figure.4.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch4_births_deaths}{{4.3}{33}{Births versus total deaths over time (mean per year, three replicates). Top row: baseline births (left) and baseline total deaths (right). Bottom row: anthropogenic-food births (left) and anthropogenic-food total deaths (right). Under the food driver, births stay high while deaths fall, and it is this widening gap between births and deaths that produces the sustained population increase seen in Figure~\ref {fig:ch4_population_all}}{figure.4.3}{}}
